{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And now, flee to your place.</b> The sage RaDaK explained in the name of his father, of blessed memory, that this is not actual flight, but like one who says 'hurry to go'; and he said that its parallel is (Isaiah 48:20): ״ברחו מכשדים״ — 'flee from Chaldea' — for Israel went out from Babylon with a high hand and with a voice of ringing song. And likewise in Jonah (Jonah 1:3): ״לברוח תרשישה״ — 'to flee unto Tarshish' — and Jonah's intention was not that he could flee from the Holy One, blessed be He, but that he should hurry to go to Tarshish; and this is why it says ״מלפני ה'״ — 'from before HaShem' — and did not say ״מפני ה'״ — 'from the face of HaShem' — as it says (Psalms 139:7): ״ואנה מפניך אברח״. And the reason he hurried to go was his intention that the Holy One, blessed be He, not give a sign and wonder to the people of Nineveh so that they would repent, and their repentance would cause Israel's destruction. And concerning this he said afterward (Jonah 4:2): ״על כן קדמתי לברוח תרשישה״ — 'therefore I hastened to flee unto Tarshish.' And there is no sign greater than the sign of the sea, for those who cast him in recognized him when he emerged from the sea, and then ״וגע הדבר אל מלך נינוה״ — 'the matter reached the king of Nineveh.' This is a fine interpretation, except that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, understood it as actual flight from the land of Israel, so that prophecy would not rest upon him and he would not go on that mission." "<b>I thought to honor you, I will honor you.</b> The reason for the doubled expression — ״אמרתי כבד״ at first, ״אכבדך״ afterward — is that in two instances, in two places, I had hope for you that you would curse them and that I would honor you on that account. But now, the third time — when your blessing of them has already been established three times and you have said, ״אורריך ארור ומברכיך ברוך״ — 'cursed be they who curse you, and blessed be they who bless you' — I see that HaShem has withheld honor from you, and from now on I despair, for I have no hope to come against them; therefore he told him to flee."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:53.858410178Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}